CDX2097 unused-partial-application control, COMPILER-69.

Compile this chapter and read the WARNINGS, not the output. There is no
test mechanism for a warning in this tree -- codex/test/errors holds no
.expected files and nothing anywhere declares a warning -- which is why
this lives here rather than in codex/test, and why the behaviour below is
measured and NOT gated (L-NOGATE).

  before (no such check, seed 05E254BFF0C46406)
    no CDX2097 anywhere

  after
    CDX2097 on 'short'

The four arms and why exactly one of them is the defect:

  short    tally applied to 2 of 3 arguments, read by nothing. The call
           never completes, so on an effectful callee the effect never
           happens. THIS is the shape COMPILER-69 exists to catch, and it
           is the shape that painted nothing in desk-cursor-arrow.
  carried  tally applied to 2 of 3 arguments and READ by 'done'. A partial
           application is a legitimate value when something consumes it,
           so a check that warns here is warning on currying.
  full     tally applied to all 3 arguments, read by print-line-uni.
  done     'carried' applied to its last argument, read by nothing. The
           call COMPLETED, so the effects happened and discarding an
           Integer result is ordinary; a check that warns here is an
           unused-variable check, which is a different unit.

A change that silences 'short' has made the check vacuous. A change that
fires on 'carried' or 'done' has made it a nuisance, which is the way a
warning gets switched off wholesale rather than fixed.
